  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "Look at this one tiny ridge, not the whole fingerprint. Here, the ridge ends. Here, another ridge splits into two. Details like these are called minutiae. A capacitive sensor maps them by measuring tiny electrical differences at many small points. The phone records which details are present and where they sit relative to one another. That is a mathematical template, not a photograph. On your next touch, the phone builds another template. It compares corresponding details, even if your finger shifted or rotated slightly. Enough matching features can unlock, too few cannot. Recognition means comparing patterns, not finding too identical pictures.",
